---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="Ferrara"
      eyebrow="The Zampiello maternal name"
      dek="Giuseppa Ferrara married Angelo Zampiello and became Francesca's mother — and until now the Zampiello page did not name her once.">

  <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:1.2rem">
    <p><strong>This page corrects an omission, and says so.</strong> The archive has a
    <a href={u("/zampiello")}>Zampiello page</a> which follows that family from Arpaia to Brisbane.
    It did not contain the word <em>Ferrara</em>. Giuseppa Ferrara is <strong>Francesca Zampiello's
    mother</strong> — a grandmother of the Brisbane generation — and she was missing from the page
    about her own daughters.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Giuseppa Ferrara, and the spelling problem in her own records</h2>
  <p class="lede">She married <strong>Angelo Zampiello</strong> — born 31 August 1895, died 25 April
  1972 — and the archive can name <strong>five of their children</strong>. The family papers write her
  <strong>Ferrara</strong>. <strong>The Italian civil records write her FERRARO</strong>, in every one
  of the three entries found so far.</p>

  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Date</th><th>How it spells her</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Death of her infant son <strong>Angelo Zampiello</strong>, Arpaia</td>
        <td class="mono">26 Apr 1935</td><td><em>parents: Angelo · <strong>Giuseppa Ferraro</strong></em></td></tr>
      <tr><td>The same death, second index entry</td><td class="mono">26 Apr 1935</td>
        <td><em>parents: Angelo Zampiello · <strong>Giuseppa Ferraro</strong></em></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Marriage of her daughter <strong>Tommasina Zampiello</strong>, Arpaia</td>
        <td class="mono">26 Aug 1939</td><td><em>parents: Angelo Zampiello · <strong>Giuseppa Ferraro</strong></em></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <p><strong>Which is her name?</strong> The archive does not decide. <em>Ferraro</em> and
  <em>Ferrara</em> are both real southern surnames, the civil clerk had one and the family kept the
  other, and a name that changes between the register and the household is a fact about the family
  worth keeping rather than tidying. Both spellings are carried, and the register indexes her under
  each.</p>

  <h2>Her children</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Child</th><th>Dates</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Tommasina Zampiello</strong> (Ruggiero)</td><td class="mono">9 Feb 1921 – 2 Jun 2004</td>
        <td>Married Michele Ruggiero. Her 1939 marriage act is one of the three records that name her mother.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Francesca Zampiello</strong> (Falco)</td><td class="mono">27 Apr 1928 – 10 Sep 2022</td>
        <td>Married <strong>Carmine Falco</strong> in 1955. <strong>Generation eight of the direct line.</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Caterina Zampiello</strong> (Falco)</td><td class="mono">—</td>
        <td>Married <strong>Raffaele Falco</strong>. <em>Two Zampiello sisters married two Falco brothers</em>,
        and both households went to Brisbane.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Angelo Zampiello</strong></td><td class="mono">1935 – 26 Apr 1935</td><td rowspan="2">
        <strong>Two infant sons, both born in 1935 and both dead on 26 April 1935.</strong> The civil
        index carries the death twice. Whether they were twins the record does not say in so many
        words — but two sons of one couple dying on one day, in one year of birth, is what it shows,
        and it is set down here rather than left out because it is hard to read.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Aniello Zampiello</strong></td><td class="mono">1935 – 26 Apr 1935</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Her own parents — where the tree contradicts itself</h2>
  <p>Two versions sit in the family tree and <strong>they do not agree</strong>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A <strong>Giuseppa Ferrara born 29 June 1912 at Arpaia</strong>, daughter of <strong>Antonio
    Ferrara</strong> and <strong>Felicia Mauro</strong>.</li>
    <li>A <strong>Caterina Ferrara</strong> entered as the mother of «Giuseppa Ferrara (Zampiello)».</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>The 1912 birth date is the problem.</strong> A woman born in June 1912 would have been
  eight years old when Tommasina was born in February 1921. So either the 1912 Giuseppa is a
  different person of the same name — Arpaia is small and the name is not rare — or the date is
  wrong. <strong>No record has been found that names Giuseppa's parents</strong>, and this archive
  will not choose between two tree entries when it has none.</p>

  <h2>The other Ferrara this archive has met</h2>
  <p>The name turns up twice more, in two different centuries, and neither is joined to Giuseppa.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>Who</th><th>What it says</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Arienzo, 1826 and 1828</strong></td><td><strong>Michele Ferrara</strong>, <em>contadino</em></td>
        <td>Buries a two-year-old son Carmine in August 1826 and a seven-month-old daughter Carmina in
        November 1828 — <strong>both on STRADA CAMELLARA</strong>, the Falco street. Read off the
        death registers act by act.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Arienzo, 1842</td><td>Angelo Ferrara, 50, <em>contadino</em></td><td>Declares on strada Croce.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Arienzo, 1817</td><td>Giovanna Ferrara</td><td>Named as a parent in a December act.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Nudgee Cemetery, Brisbane</strong></td><td><strong>Paolino Ferrara</strong> and
        <strong>Elisa Ferrara</strong></td>
        <td>Buried <strong>1994 and 2021 in adjoining niches, CHAP-070-R2 and R1</strong>. A third,
        Antonio Ferrara, cremated 2019. The archive's own note on the Elisa record reads:
        <em>«Ferrara is also the Zampiello maternal surname; connection untested.»</em>
        <strong>It is still untested.</strong> Chapel 070 is not the chapel the Falco lie in — they are
        in 082 — so proximity is not evidence either way.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>What would settle it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Giuseppa's own birth act at Arpaia</strong>, probably in the 1890s rather than 1912.
    It would name her parents and end the contradiction above.</li>
    <li><strong>Her marriage to Angelo Zampiello</strong>, probably about 1920. It names both fathers,
    and it would also fix the spelling in the hand of a clerk who had to ask her.</li>
    <li><strong>The Arpaia death registers</strong> for the 1930s, which this archive has not read act
    by act, and which hold the two boys of April 1935 in full.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.2rem">Every Ferrara record is listed with its source at
  <a href={u("/register") + "?q=Ferrara"}>the register →</a>, and the family it married into is at
  <a href={u("/zampiello")}>Zampiello</a>.</p>
</Base>
